  <p class="secondary">Foundational paper · 1965</p>
  <h1>Self-Consistent Equations Including Exchange and Correlation Effects</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Kohn and Sham turn a density variational principle into one-electron equations by preserving an auxiliary noninteracting kinetic energy exactly and placing the remaining many-body contribution in an exchange–correlation functional. The main text develops practical approximations; a note added in proof states the exact formal version that became the modern Kohn–Sham foundation.
  </p>
  <p class="paper-meta">
    W. Kohn and L. J. Sham, <cite>Physical Review</cite> 140, A1133–A1138 (1965). <a href="https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.140.A1133">DOI 10.1103/PhysRev.140.A1133</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The paper begins from a practical impasse</h2>
  <p>
    Hohenberg and Kohn had shown that a ground-state density variational principle exists, but existence did not make the universal functional calculable. The 1965 paper asks how knowledge of the homogeneous interacting electron gas can be used for real inhomogeneous systems—atoms, molecules, solids, metals, and alloys—without returning to a full many-electron wavefunction calculation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The authors’ target is ground-state energetics and density, not an exact spectrum of independent physical electrons. They seek self-consistent equations resembling Hartree or Hartree–Fock equations while including exchange and correlation through an effective potential. That historical motivation matters: the paper is solving the missing computational closure of density theory, not inventing Bloch bands or declaring that real electrons cease to interact.
  </p>

  <figure class="paper-argument" aria-labelledby="ks-route-caption">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Start</strong><span>an exact but unknown density functional</span></li>
      <li><strong>Extract</strong><span>a noninteracting kinetic functional represented by orbitals</span></li>
      <li><strong>Approximate</strong><span>the remaining exchange–correlation energy from the uniform gas</span></li>
      <li><strong>Close</strong><span>density-dependent one-electron equations self-consistently</span></li>
    </ol>
    <figcaption id="ks-route-caption">The one-electron equations are a density construction inside an interacting ground-state theory. Their operator depends on the density that their solutions must reproduce.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Section II first reorganizes the unknown functional</h2>
  <p>
    The paper inherits a total-energy expression in which the external potential and classical Coulomb energy are explicit, while a universal functional <math class="math-inline" aria-label="G of n"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[n]</annotation></semantics></math> contains everything else:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The density energy equals external energy plus classical Hartree energy plus the universal remainder G">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>∬</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[n]=\int v(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^3\mathbf&#123;r&#125;+\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;2&#125;\iint\frac&#123;n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;')&#125;&#123;|\mathbf&#123;r&#125;-\mathbf&#123;r&#125;'|&#125;\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^3\mathbf&#123;r&#125;\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^3\mathbf&#123;r&#125;'+G[n].</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The equation is not yet a Kohn–Sham equation. It is an energy ledger. The first term identifies the particular physical system through <math class="math-inline" aria-label="v of r"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)</annotation></semantics></math>; the second is the classical electrostatic self-energy of the density; <math class="math-inline" aria-label="G of n"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[n]</annotation></semantics></math> remains unknown.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Kohn and Sham, Eq. (2.1), p. A1133.</p>

  <h2>The decisive split keeps an orbital kinetic energy</h2>
  <p>
    Kohn and Sham next divide <math class="math-inline" aria-label="G"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> into the kinetic energy of an auxiliary noninteracting system that yields the density and a remainder:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="G equals the noninteracting kinetic energy functional plus exchange-correlation energy, approximated locally from the uniform gas">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.5em" rowspacing="0.65em">
          <mtr><mtd><mi>G</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>xc</mtext></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>xc</mtext></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>≈</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>∫</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mtext>xc</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[n]=T_&#123;\mathrm&#123;s&#125;&#125;[n]+E_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;[n],\qquad E_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;[n]\approx\int n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)\varepsilon_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;(n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;))\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^3\mathbf&#123;r&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Read the first line as a definition of responsibility. <math class="math-inline" aria-label="T sub s"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_&#123;\mathrm&#123;s&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is not the kinetic energy of the interacting wavefunction. It is the kinetic energy of a noninteracting reference system chosen to reproduce the density. The remainder <math class="math-inline" aria-label="E x c"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>xc</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is whatever must supplement that auxiliary kinetic energy and the explicit Hartree term. In the exact decomposition it therefore includes exchange, nonclassical Coulomb correlation, and the difference between interacting and auxiliary kinetic energies. It is not merely a small empirical “correction.”
  </p>
  <p>
    The second line is where the paper makes its main local approximation. <math class="math-inline" aria-label="epsilon x c of n"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mtext>xc</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is the exchange–correlation energy per electron of a homogeneous electron gas at density <math class="math-inline" aria-label="n"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. The approximation assigns each point in an inhomogeneous system the value from a uniform gas with the same local density. Modern language calls this the local-density approximation, but the paper’s derivation should be read before importing the later name.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Kohn and Sham, Eqs. (2.2)–(2.3), pp. A1133–A1134.</p>

  <h2>Stationarity turns the density functional into orbital equations</h2>
  <p>
    The auxiliary kinetic energy becomes tractable because it can be represented by one-electron orbitals. Varying the energy at fixed electron number gives a local effective potential. In the paper’s notation,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The local Kohn-Sham equations and density reconstructed from occupied orbitals">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.5em" rowspacing="0.65em">
          <mtr><mtd><mo>[</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>∫</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>xc</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><munderover><mo largeop="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>xc</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mtext>xc</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-\tfrac&#123;1&#125;&#123;2&#125;\nabla^2+v(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)+\int\tfrac&#123;n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;')&#125;&#123;|\mathbf&#123;r&#125;-\mathbf&#123;r&#125;'|&#125;\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^3\mathbf&#123;r&#125;'+\mu_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;(n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;))]\psi_i(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)=\varepsilon_i\psi_i(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;),\qquad n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)=\sum_&#123;i=1&#125;^N|\psi_i(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)|^2,\qquad \mu_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;(n)=\frac&#123;\mathrm&#123;d&#125;[n\varepsilon_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;(n)]&#125;&#123;\mathrm&#123;d&#125;n&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Read the operator from left to right. The Laplacian supplies the auxiliary one-electron kinetic term. The external potential identifies the physical environment. The density integral is the Hartree potential. The final local term is the derivative of the uniform-gas exchange–correlation energy used in the approximation. The orbitals produce a new density through the second line.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why self-consistency appears: the operator needed to obtain the orbitals depends on the density made from those same orbitals. The paper explicitly describes the cycle—begin with a trial density, build the effective potential, solve the one-electron equations, construct a new density, and repeat. Self-consistency is the solution structure of the density-dependent equations; it is not itself the exchange–correlation approximation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The eigenvalues <math class="math-inline" aria-label="epsilon i"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_i</annotation></semantics></math> arise as orbital stationary-equation multipliers. The paper does not prove that all of them are exact charged or neutral excitation energies. Likewise, in a periodic system these orbitals can be Bloch states because the effective one-particle Hamiltonian is periodic; the Bloch theorem and the existence of bands come from translation symmetry, as <a href={href('/core/part-iii/')}>Core Part III</a> established before DFT entered.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Kohn and Sham, Eqs. (2.4)–(2.9), p. A1134.</p>

  <h2>The eigenvalue sum is not the total energy</h2>
  <p>
    Because the effective eigenproblem already contains the Hartree and exchange–correlation potentials, simply adding its occupied eigenvalues would count some energy contributions incorrectly. The paper therefore gives a separate total-energy expression:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The total energy corrects the occupied eigenvalue sum for Hartree double counting and exchange-correlation potential contributions">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo largeop="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>∬</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mtext>xc</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>xc</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\sum_&#123;i=1&#125;^N\varepsilon_i-\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;2&#125;\iint\frac&#123;n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;')&#125;&#123;|\mathbf&#123;r&#125;-\mathbf&#123;r&#125;'|&#125;\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^3\mathbf&#123;r&#125;\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^3\mathbf&#123;r&#125;'+\int n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)[\varepsilon_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;(n)-\mu_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;(n)]\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^3\mathbf&#123;r&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The first subtraction removes half of the Hartree contribution already embedded in the orbital eigenvalues. The last integral replaces the exchange–correlation potential contribution in that sum with the exchange–correlation energy functional used by the theory. The consequence is conceptual as well as algebraic: orbital eigenvalues and the ground-state total energy are different kinds of objects.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Kohn and Sham, Eq. (2.10), p. A1134.</p>

  <h2>The main approximation has two instructive limiting cases</h2>
  <p>
    Kohn and Sham do not present the local approximation as uniformly reliable. They identify two limits in which their procedure becomes exact in the paper’s analysis. For a slowly varying density, the leading local term of the exchange–correlation gradient expansion is appropriate. At high density, exchange and correlation are small relative to kinetic energy; retaining the full auxiliary kinetic functional rather than replacing it by a Thomas–Fermi kinetic expression is therefore decisive.
  </p>
  <p>
    The limiting-case reading exposes why orbitals are more than decoration. If <math class="math-inline" aria-label="T s"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_&#123;\mathrm&#123;s&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> were also localized into a density-only Thomas–Fermi form, the method would produce an unphysical nuclear divergence and miss quantum shell oscillations. Keeping an orbital representation captures those kinetic effects even while exchange and correlation are approximated locally.
  </p>
  <p>
    The authors then give a candid system-by-system discussion. They expect the approximation to work better near high-density nuclei and in slowly varying bulk regions than in atomic surfaces and molecular overlap regions. They explicitly warn that the latter limitation threatens an accurate account of chemical binding. That warning belongs to the paper’s argument; later success of density-functional approximations should not be retroactively written into the 1965 evidence.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Kohn and Sham, discussion following Eqs. (2.10)–(2.20), pp. A1134–A1135.</p>

  <h2>A second branch keeps exact exchange nonlocal</h2>
  <p>
    Section II.B does not merely repeat the local scheme. It proposes an alternative Hartree–Fock-like route: use an orbital-dependent exact-exchange energy together with a local correlation approximation. The resulting one-electron equation contains a nonlocal exchange operator. This branch shows that the paper already distinguishes two kinds of auxiliary operator—local density-dependent and nonlocal orbital-dependent—even though modern terminology such as generalized Kohn–Sham theory came later.
  </p>
  <p>
    The distinction remains important now. “Kohn–Sham-like orbitals” do not guarantee that every approximation produces one multiplicative local potential. Changing the exchange–correlation approximation can change the mathematical class of the one-particle operator, not merely a numerical setting.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Kohn and Sham, Sec. II.B, Eqs. (2.21)–(2.25), pp. A1135–A1136.</p>

  <h2>Finite temperature, spin, and the paper’s historical scope</h2>
  <p>
    Sections III and IV extend the authors’ density approach to finite-temperature thermodynamics and spin susceptibility. These are real parts of the source, not foundations of the zero-temperature equations above. The finite-temperature section works with an ensemble and temperature-dependent occupations; the spin section works with the density together with a magnetization density and an effective magnetic field. Later finite-temperature DFT and spin-density-functional formulations sharpened these ideas.
  </p>
  <p>
    The paper does not report a modern electronic-structure calculation with a code, basis set, plane-wave cutoff, pseudopotential, Brillouin-zone mesh, or convergence study. Those quantities are therefore <strong>not reported</strong>, and they are not needed to follow the formal derivation. Part V of the Core explains where such numerical layers enter once these continuous equations are implemented.
  </p>

  <h2>The note added in proof states the exact formal construction</h2>
  <p>
    The paper’s final page contains a historically decisive qualification. The main text inserted the local approximation for <math class="math-inline" aria-label="E x c"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>xc</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> before deriving its equations. The note observes that one can instead retain the exact functional in the same variational structure and define
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The exact exchange-correlation potential is the functional derivative of the exact exchange-correlation energy">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>xc</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>xc</mtext></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)=\frac&#123;\delta E_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;[n]&#125;&#123;\delta n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Read this functional derivative as a local sensitivity: make an infinitesimal change to the density near <math class="math-inline" aria-label="position vector r"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;r&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>, and it tells how the total exchange–correlation energy changes to first order. That energy sensitivity is the potential that enters the one-electron equation. With the exact functional, the effective one-electron equations are formally density-equivalent to the interacting ground-state problem and reproduce the exact density and total energy under the construction’s representability assumptions. An explicit potential is still available only if the functional is known. This is the bridge from exact density-functional principle to Kohn–Sham practice: the auxiliary construction is exact in form, while every practical calculation must choose an approximation for the unknown exchange–correlation functional.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Kohn and Sham, “Note added in proof,” p. A1138.</p>

  <h2>What the paper established—and what it did not</h2>
  <dl class="source-map">
    <dt>Established as a construction</dt>
    <dd>An auxiliary noninteracting kinetic functional can be represented by orbitals that reproduce the density, producing density-dependent one-electron equations and a self-consistent closure.</dd>
    <dt>Approximated in the main text</dt>
    <dd>The exchange–correlation energy is supplied locally from the homogeneous electron gas, with a separate nonlocal exact-exchange-plus-local-correlation branch.</dd>
    <dt>Clarified in the note added in proof</dt>
    <dd>The same orbital construction can be written formally with the exact exchange–correlation functional; practical closure still requires knowing or approximating that functional.</dd>
    <dt>Not proved</dt>
    <dd>That the auxiliary electrons are literal noninteracting physical electrons, that all orbital eigenvalues are general excitation energies, that a Kohn–Sham band gap equals a fundamental or optical gap, or that self-consistent convergence establishes physical accuracy.</dd>
  </dl>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Modern perspective.</strong> Today “exact Kohn–Sham DFT” usually refers to the exact density-preserving auxiliary construction, with admissible-density and noninteracting-representability conditions made explicit. LDA, GGA, meta-GGA, hybrids, and other practical choices are approximations within or extensions of that construction; they are not synonyms for the Hohenberg–Kohn theorem.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>How this paper fits the Atlas</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={href('/core/part-iv/')}>Core Part IV · Density Functional Theory</a> gives the modern sequence from the density theorem through exact Kohn–Sham decomposition, approximation, and nonlinearity. <a href={href('/theory/kohn-sham-density-functional-theory/')}>Foundations · Kohn–Sham Density-Functional Theory</a> repairs the formal orbital construction, while <a href={href('/core/part-v/')}>Core Part V</a> explains how the continuous equations become finite calculations. For the theorem that precedes this paper, return to <a href={href('/reading/literature/hohenberg-kohn-1964/')}>Hohenberg and Kohn 1964</a>.
  </p>

